1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,162
WWW.MY-SUBS.CO

1
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:45,340
Is there any news of Sybil?

2
00:00:45,340 --> 00:00:48,580
She's still not coming. She
insists they can't afford it.

3
00:00:48,580 --> 00:00:50,580
Mr Travis, can we move forward?

4
00:00:51,140 --> 00:00:53,940
If I could just ask you to
come down the aisle again?

5
00:00:55,220 --> 00:00:57,220
Can we get the troops organised?

6
00:00:57,300 --> 00:00:59,300
That means me.

7
00:00:59,620 --> 00:01:03,220
It seems rather hard on poor old
Travis when he's doing all the work,

8
00:01:03,220 --> 00:01:05,060
but the Archbishop gets the glory.

9
00:01:05,060 --> 00:01:07,460
Papa was the one who wanted
a prince of the church.

10
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:09,220
I'd have settled for Travis.

11
00:01:09,220 --> 00:01:12,460
Is there really no way we can get
Sybil over? It seems ridiculous.

12
00:01:12,460 --> 00:01:14,620
On the contrary, it's a relief.

13
00:01:14,620 --> 00:01:18,140
Branson is still an object
of fascination for the county.

14
00:01:18,140 --> 00:01:22,500
We'll ask him here when we can prepare
the servants and manage it gently.

15
00:01:22,660 --> 00:01:24,900
He's making a problem where none exists.

16
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:28,060
No-one could care less whether
Branson's at the wedding or not.

17
00:01:28,260 --> 00:01:30,820
You must think country life
more exciting than it is

18
00:01:30,820 --> 00:01:34,980
if you imagine people don't care when an
earl's daughter runs off with the chauffeur.

19
00:01:34,980 --> 00:01:38,740
She has run off with the chauffeur
and they'll have to get used to it.

20
00:01:38,740 --> 00:01:42,780
Mr Travis, are we ready? Any
moment, Your Grace. Any moment.

21
00:01:44,220 --> 00:01:46,220
Can we... Please.

22
00:02:00,100 --> 00:02:03,020
That treacle tart just hit the
spot. Thank you, Mrs Patmore.

23
00:02:03,020 --> 00:02:05,860
So, Mrs Hughes and Anna are
getting the place ready to let?

24
00:02:05,860 --> 00:02:07,700
That is the plan.

25
00:02:07,700 --> 00:02:09,980
I'm surprised Anna held on to that house.

26
00:02:10,380 --> 00:02:13,100
I thought they'd confiscated
the profits of murder.

27
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:16,260
Mr Bates had the wisdom to
transfer it to her before the trial.

28
00:02:16,260 --> 00:02:18,220
I don't think I'd have allowed it.

29
00:02:18,220 --> 00:02:21,020
Then we must be grateful you
were not the presiding judge.

30
00:02:21,540 --> 00:02:25,540
I still think it's funny, given
that he's a convicted murderer.

31
00:02:25,540 --> 00:02:29,300
May I remind you, Mr Barrow, that in
this house Mr Bates is a wronged man

32
00:02:29,300 --> 00:02:32,860
seeking justice. If you have any
problems with that definition,

33
00:02:32,860 --> 00:02:34,860
I suggest you eat in the yard.

34
00:02:42,100 --> 00:02:46,340
I suppose you agree with Robert. Not
for the first time, you suppose wrongly.

35
00:02:47,860 --> 00:02:51,500
The family must never be
a topic of conversation.

36
00:02:51,500 --> 00:02:54,780
I'm afraid Sybil's already made
the Crawleys a permanent topic.

37
00:02:54,780 --> 00:02:56,820
All the more reason.

38
00:02:56,820 --> 00:02:59,140
If we can show the county
he can behave normally,

39
00:02:59,140 --> 00:03:01,020
they will soon lose interest in him.

40
00:03:01,020 --> 00:03:03,620
And I shall make sure he behaves normally

41
00:03:03,620 --> 00:03:06,860
because I shall hold his hand
on the radiator until he does.

44
00:03:14,460 --> 00:03:18,660
Oh, here we go. And why should
he be normal as you call it?

45
00:03:18,660 --> 00:03:21,820
I say he should come
here and fight his corner.

46
00:03:21,820 --> 00:03:23,940
I like a man of strong beliefs.

47
00:03:25,100 --> 00:03:26,940
I think I'll send them the money.

48
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:30,660
Please don't. Robert's expressly
forbidden it. He'd be furious.

49
00:03:33,020 --> 00:03:34,860
But it can't be as bad as... 

50
00:03:36,180 --> 00:03:38,940
Look, I'll come and see you, tomorrow.

51
00:03:39,380 --> 00:03:41,380
No, I insist.

52
00:03:41,700 --> 00:03:43,700
Right. Goodbye.

53
00:03:46,260 --> 00:03:48,140
Papa... What's the matter?

54
00:03:48,860 --> 00:03:51,660
Nothing's the matter.
What should be the matter?

55
00:03:58,300 --> 00:04:00,780
How was London? We got it all done.

56
00:04:00,780 --> 00:04:04,060
But I couldn't have managed
without my helper. Have you eaten?

57
00:04:04,060 --> 00:04:05,820
We had a bite on the train.

58
00:04:05,820 --> 00:04:07,900
Well, sit down anyway. Have a cup of tea.

59
00:04:08,460 --> 00:04:13,460
I'll start on the final list
for the wedding tomorrow morning.

60
00:04:13,460 --> 00:04:16,820
I've got the last of the wine
deliveries coming on Tuesday.

61
00:04:16,820 --> 00:04:19,940
How will you manage
without a footman? I agree.

62
00:04:19,940 --> 00:04:21,900
But I haven't time to find one now.

63
00:04:21,900 --> 00:04:25,900
I've had a letter from my sister
asking after a job for her son and -

64
00:04:25,900 --> 00:04:29,380
Miss O'Brien, we are about
to host a society wedding.

65
00:04:29,380 --> 00:04:32,300
I have no time for
training young hobbledehoys.

66
00:04:35,300 --> 00:04:37,300
Her ladyship is ringing.

67
00:04:43,780 --> 00:04:45,660
Well, I don't see why not.

68
00:04:45,660 --> 00:04:48,540
I'll ask his lordship
when... There you are.

69
00:04:48,540 --> 00:04:50,940
So I'll ask you now. Ask me what?

70
00:04:50,940 --> 00:04:54,180
Carson's in need of a footman
and O'Brien has a candidate.

71
00:04:54,180 --> 00:04:57,020
Alfred. Alfred Nugent, my
lord. He's a good worker.

72
00:04:57,020 --> 00:04:59,020
I think it sounds perfect.

73
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:04,140
Robert? Whatever you say. My dear,
I have to go up to London tomorrow.

74
00:05:04,140 --> 00:05:06,980
I'll be catching the early
train. That's very sudden.

75
00:05:06,980 --> 00:05:10,060
Do you want them to open the
house? No, I'll come straight back.

76
00:05:10,060 --> 00:05:13,100
What are you going for? It's
nothing to bother you with.

77
00:05:27,860 --> 00:05:30,100
It's all there, every entry.

78
00:05:32,140 --> 00:05:35,180
Where did you find the
book? Behind the bureau.

79
00:05:35,180 --> 00:05:37,380
We moved it out to clean and there it was.

80
00:05:38,500 --> 00:05:40,500
Vera must have dropped it or something.

81
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:42,380
So what do you want me to do?

82
00:05:42,380 --> 00:05:44,380
Make notes on all the names.

83
00:05:44,940 --> 00:05:46,940
Close friend, relation.

84
00:05:47,180 --> 00:05:49,180
Workmate, tradesman, and so on.

85
00:05:49,380 --> 00:05:53,060
Then I'll copy those and I'll send
them with the book to Mr Murray.

86
00:05:53,820 --> 00:05:56,980
Haven't you anything
better to do? I have not.

87
00:05:57,740 --> 00:06:02,460
Because I'd rather work to get you free
than dine with the King at Buckingham Palace.

88
00:06:05,780 --> 00:06:07,780
So what news have you got?

89
00:06:08,500 --> 00:06:10,500
What news could I have in here?

90
00:06:11,420 --> 00:06:13,380
Oh, I've acquired a new cellmate.

91
00:06:13,380 --> 00:06:15,380
To be honest, I'm not sure about him.

92
00:06:16,820 --> 00:06:20,180
Well, just remember what
my mother used to say.

93
00:06:20,700 --> 00:06:22,700
Never make an enemy by accident.

94
00:06:24,340 --> 00:06:28,060
Now, do you think you can get the
notes done before my next visit?

95
00:06:29,340 --> 00:06:31,100
I don't see what can come of it.

96
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:33,500
Probably nothing.

97
00:06:33,500 --> 00:06:35,900
And my next idea will
probably lead to nothing.

98
00:06:35,900 --> 00:06:37,900
And the next, and the next.

99
00:06:38,300 --> 00:06:40,780
But one day something will occur to us.

100
00:06:41,140 --> 00:06:43,020
And we'll follow it up.

101
00:06:43,020 --> 00:06:45,140
And the case against you will crumble.

102
00:06:46,860 --> 00:06:48,580
Do you never doubt?

103
00:06:48,580 --> 00:06:50,580
For just one minute.

104
00:06:51,700 --> 00:06:55,260
I wouldn't blame you. No.

105
00:06:55,860 --> 00:06:58,940
I don't doubt that the sun
will rise in the east either.

106
00:07:03,380 --> 00:07:05,380
You're too tall to be a footman.

107
00:07:05,940 --> 00:07:08,740
No footman should be over six foot one.

108
00:07:08,740 --> 00:07:11,940
That can't be, can it, since
he's already been taken on?

109
00:07:12,780 --> 00:07:16,900
Well, what have you done? I was a hotel
waiter after I was discharged from the army.

110
00:07:16,900 --> 00:07:21,340
But they've cut back. I think
that shows real initiative.

111
00:07:21,740 --> 00:07:24,180
I suppose he can speak for himself.

112
00:07:24,180 --> 00:07:26,180
Why? Is he on trial?

113
00:07:26,820 --> 00:07:30,380
This isn't an interview, is it?
Not when he's already got the job.

114
00:07:30,380 --> 00:07:32,660
No, it is not an interview, Miss O'Brien,

115
00:07:32,660 --> 00:07:35,860
but he is on trial and if he
cannot match our standards,

116
00:07:35,860 --> 00:07:38,740
he will be found guilty.
I mean to try, Mr Carson.

117
00:07:38,740 --> 00:07:40,740
As long as you do.

118
00:07:40,740 --> 00:07:42,860
Right, go upstairs and get settled in.

119
00:07:43,180 --> 00:07:46,540
Your aunt will hopefully
find you a livery that fits.

120
00:07:52,220 --> 00:07:56,460
Just at the start, so we've a
place to sleep after the honeymoon.

121
00:07:56,460 --> 00:07:58,340
You can't object to that.

122
00:07:58,340 --> 00:08:00,180
No, it's nice of them.

123
00:08:00,580 --> 00:08:04,300
Though I doubt I'll get used to taking
you to bed with your father watching.

124
00:08:04,300 --> 00:08:08,460
He's so relieved we're getting married, he
wouldn't mind if you carried me up naked.

125
00:08:08,460 --> 00:08:10,460
Careful. I might try it.

126
00:08:13,020 --> 00:08:15,180
I don't want to move to London or anything.

127
00:08:15,180 --> 00:08:17,580
I'm not kicking against the traces.

128
00:08:17,580 --> 00:08:19,380
Just testing their strengths.

129
00:08:19,380 --> 00:08:21,380
I want us to get to know each other.

130
00:08:22,420 --> 00:08:25,900
To learn about who we both are,
without everybody being there.

131
00:08:26,740 --> 00:08:28,700
Well, it's quite a big house.

132
00:08:28,700 --> 00:08:30,740
It's a lovely house.

133
00:08:30,740 --> 00:08:33,140
It's your home and I
want it to be my home too.

134
00:08:34,700 --> 00:08:36,700
Just not quite yet.

135
00:08:45,420 --> 00:08:48,660
Chancery Lane. Yes, sir.

136
00:08:52,780 --> 00:08:55,500
I've spoken to Frobisher and Curran

137
00:08:55,500 --> 00:08:59,260
and, since I am a trustee,
should the estate ever need one,

138
00:08:59,260 --> 00:09:01,380
we felt I ought to be the one to tell you.

139
00:09:02,020 --> 00:09:03,900
You make it sound very serious.

140
00:09:03,900 --> 00:09:07,540
I'm expressing myself badly
if you think it is not serious.

141
00:09:09,540 --> 00:09:12,460
Why did we invest so much?

142
00:09:12,460 --> 00:09:15,380
Lord Grantham, it was you
who insisted we should.

143
00:09:15,380 --> 00:09:17,540
If you remember, we advised against it.

144
00:09:17,540 --> 00:09:20,860
But war would mean a huge
expansion of railways everywhere.

145
00:09:20,860 --> 00:09:22,780
Every forecast was certain.

146
00:09:22,780 --> 00:09:25,340
Rail shares were bound to make a fortune.

147
00:09:25,340 --> 00:09:28,860
Many did. But your principal
holding, which was very large indeed,

148
00:09:28,860 --> 00:09:31,540
was in the Canadian Grand Trunk Line.

149
00:09:31,540 --> 00:09:35,420
It was the main railway in British
North America, for God's sake.

150
00:09:35,420 --> 00:09:38,540
It wasn't just me. Everyone
said we couldn't lose.

151
00:09:39,620 --> 00:09:42,380
We knew hard times were
coming for estates like Downton

152
00:09:42,380 --> 00:09:45,420
and this investment would make
it safe for the rest of time.

153
00:09:45,420 --> 00:09:47,580
Charles Hays was the presiding genius

154
00:09:47,580 --> 00:09:50,020
and since he died, the
management has not... 

155
00:09:51,020 --> 00:09:54,820
The fact is, the company is
about to be declared bankrupt.

156
00:09:55,140 --> 00:09:59,260
And the line will be absorbed into
the Canadian National Railways scheme.

157
00:10:03,100 --> 00:10:06,020
Are you really telling me
that all the money has gone?

158
00:10:07,180 --> 00:10:10,700
I'm afraid so. The lion's
share of Cora's fortune?

159
00:10:20,540 --> 00:10:22,540
I won't give in, Murray.

160
00:10:23,420 --> 00:10:26,300
I've sacrificed too much
to Downton to give in now.

161
00:10:27,020 --> 00:10:28,980
I refuse to be the failure,

162
00:10:28,980 --> 00:10:32,300
the earl who dropped the
torch and let the flame go out.

163
00:10:32,300 --> 00:10:36,540
I hate to state the obvious, but if
there's not enough money to run it... 

164
00:10:37,180 --> 00:10:39,020
Downton must go.

165
00:10:39,020 --> 00:10:42,100
Unless you break it up
and sell it off piecemeal.

166
00:10:42,100 --> 00:10:46,460
I couldn't do that. I have a
duty beyond saving my own skin.

167
00:10:47,260 --> 00:10:50,740
The estate must be a major
employer and support the house

168
00:10:50,740 --> 00:10:53,100
or there's no point to it. To any of it.

169
00:11:05,958 --> 00:11:07,958
Morning.

170
00:11:11,398 --> 00:11:13,398
Lady Edith... 

171
00:11:19,478 --> 00:11:21,478
Hello.

172
00:11:21,798 --> 00:11:25,318
What are you doing here? I'm meeting
a train, but I'm too early. Oh.

173
00:11:25,318 --> 00:11:28,238
Oh. I mustn't hold you
up. I'm not doing anything.

174
00:11:28,838 --> 00:11:30,998
I thought I'd get away from wedding panic.

175
00:11:31,758 --> 00:11:35,038
Don't you like weddings?
Don't be silly. Of course I do.

176
00:11:35,438 --> 00:11:38,758
Only I've talked of clothes
and flowers and food and guests

177
00:11:38,758 --> 00:11:40,758
until I'm blue in the face.

178
00:11:41,038 --> 00:11:44,718
Yes, weddings can be reminders
of one's loneliness, can't they?

179
00:11:45,238 --> 00:11:47,158
Sorry. I don't know why I said that.

180
00:11:47,158 --> 00:11:48,958
So, how's it going?

181
00:11:48,958 --> 00:11:52,278
Is your grandmother coming over
from New York for it? She is.

182
00:11:52,558 --> 00:11:54,598
And Sybil... Is she here yet?

183
00:11:54,598 --> 00:11:57,958
As a matter of fact, she wasn't
coming. But I think she is now.

184
00:11:57,958 --> 00:11:59,798
Mary had a letter this morning.

185
00:11:59,798 --> 00:12:02,798
Papa doesn't know yet. He will be pleased.

186
00:12:02,798 --> 00:12:04,838
I do hope so.

187
00:12:06,478 --> 00:12:09,798
So you'll live at the big house
when you're back from honeymoon?

188
00:12:09,798 --> 00:12:11,798
Not live. Stay.

189
00:12:12,198 --> 00:12:14,678
We'll stay there until
we decide where to go.

190
00:12:15,038 --> 00:12:17,958
It'll be on the estate, I
should think, or in the village.

191
00:12:17,958 --> 00:12:19,958
Not here? No.

192
00:12:20,478 --> 00:12:23,838
But I shall expect you and Mrs
Bird to look after Mrs Crawley.

193
00:12:27,638 --> 00:12:29,678
You'll not be taking me with you, sir?

194
00:12:29,918 --> 00:12:34,278
Only I thought you'd be needing a
proper valet, once you're married.

195
00:12:35,238 --> 00:12:38,918
I've always thought of you as a
butler who helps out as a valet -

196
00:12:38,918 --> 00:12:41,838
not the other way round. I'd
be happy to be a valet, sir.

197
00:12:41,838 --> 00:12:45,238
Especially in the big house. We
won't be at the big house for long.

198
00:12:45,878 --> 00:12:49,678
To be honest, Molesley, I want to
live more simply after the wedding.

199
00:12:50,998 --> 00:12:54,438
And besides, Mother
absolutely relies on you.

200
00:12:55,278 --> 00:12:57,878
Well, that's very nice
to hear, sir. Thank you.

201
00:12:59,918 --> 00:13:01,798
You must be exhausted, my lord.

202
00:13:01,798 --> 00:13:04,438
You can't have spent more
than two hours in London.

203
00:13:04,438 --> 00:13:08,358
It was sufficient. The new footman
arrived while you were gone.

204
00:13:08,838 --> 00:13:10,878
What?

205
00:13:10,878 --> 00:13:14,158
Yes, he got the cable this
morning and came straight over.

206
00:13:15,278 --> 00:13:17,118
Very eager.

207
00:13:17,118 --> 00:13:19,038
And very tall.

208
00:13:19,038 --> 00:13:21,038
But when did... 

209
00:13:21,358 --> 00:13:23,358
Never mind.

210
00:13:25,278 --> 00:13:27,438
Did you know about the new footman?

211
00:13:27,438 --> 00:13:29,438
Of course. He's already here.

212
00:13:29,598 --> 00:13:32,758
Why did no-one tell me? What do you mean?

213
00:13:32,758 --> 00:13:35,038
We talked about it last night. In my room.

214
00:13:35,238 --> 00:13:38,718
Well, nobody else must be
taken on. Absolutely no-one.

215
00:13:38,718 --> 00:13:40,718
Until things are settled.

216
00:13:40,918 --> 00:13:42,918
What things?

217
00:13:43,278 --> 00:13:46,438
How's the wedding going? I
suppose it's costing the earth?

218
00:13:46,438 --> 00:13:48,758
Mary was never going to marry on the cheap.

219
00:13:48,758 --> 00:13:51,598
Oh, no. Nothing must be done on the cheap.

220
00:13:54,478 --> 00:13:56,318
I feel quite nervous. Don't be.

221
00:13:56,318 --> 00:13:58,878
You've got the skill and
you've got the willingness.

222
00:13:58,878 --> 00:14:01,118
But he hasn't got the
experience. He's right.

223
00:14:01,118 --> 00:14:05,718
Pay no attention. You've a nice
manner. You're not vain like Thomas.

224
00:14:05,718 --> 00:14:07,718
They'll like that.

225
00:14:10,518 --> 00:14:12,478
What's the matter with you? I'm fed up.

226
00:14:12,478 --> 00:14:16,078
They promised me promotion. She
said they'd get a new kitchen maid

227
00:14:16,078 --> 00:14:17,878
and I'd be Mrs Patmore's assistant.

228
00:14:17,878 --> 00:14:20,638
If they promised, you should
withdraw your services.

229
00:14:20,638 --> 00:14:23,598
What do you mean? Like go on strike?

230
00:14:23,598 --> 00:14:25,598
But don't say I put you up to it.

231
00:14:29,078 --> 00:14:32,638
But what was in the letter? Just
that Sybil's coming after all.

232
00:14:32,638 --> 00:14:34,758
She'll be here on Wednesday for dinner.

233
00:14:34,758 --> 00:14:38,278
Will she be coming alone?
Don't make trouble, Mother.

234
00:14:39,558 --> 00:14:42,718
Can I do it? If you
wish, my lady. Of course.

235
00:14:43,478 --> 00:14:47,598
Are you really that tall? Yes, my lady.

236
00:14:48,478 --> 00:14:50,838
I thought you might have
been walking on stilts.

237
00:14:52,118 --> 00:14:54,038
When does Grandmama arrive?

238
00:14:54,038 --> 00:14:57,598
She gets into Liverpool on the 15th
- the day before the wedding

239
00:14:57,598 --> 00:15:00,238
I'm so looking forward to
seeing your mother again.

240
00:15:00,238 --> 00:15:04,038
When I'm with her I'm reminded
of the virtues of the English.

241
00:15:04,518 --> 00:15:07,158
But isn't she American? Exactly.

242
00:15:07,878 --> 00:15:09,758
Can I help myself?

243
00:15:09,758 --> 00:15:11,678
You want to as well, my lord?

244
00:15:11,678 --> 00:15:15,078
To be honest, I think you'll
find that we all do it as well.

245
00:15:15,958 --> 00:15:19,198
What do you think you're doing?
You're not in a hotel now.

246
00:15:21,718 --> 00:15:23,718
Did you train in a hotel?

247
00:15:23,718 --> 00:15:25,718
I did, ma'am.

248
00:15:26,158 --> 00:15:28,278
That will be useful. Won't it, Carson?

249
00:15:29,998 --> 00:15:32,038
Are you all set for the wedding?

250
00:15:32,038 --> 00:15:35,318
Of course he is. Carson's
motto is, 'Be prepared.'

251
00:15:35,558 --> 00:15:38,078
I'm afraid Baden-Powell has stolen it.

252
00:15:38,398 --> 00:15:40,398
But you have all the help you need?

253
00:15:40,718 --> 00:15:44,198
Well, I wouldn't fight the idea
of a second footman, my lady.

254
00:15:45,998 --> 00:15:50,078
I sometimes think it's time
we lived in a simpler way.

255
00:15:50,078 --> 00:15:52,438
I agree. Much cattle, much care.

256
00:15:52,438 --> 00:15:54,518
Always supposing we have the choice.

257
00:15:54,518 --> 00:15:58,078
Oh, don't say that. It's our
job to provide employment.

258
00:15:58,558 --> 00:16:00,638
An aristocrat with no servants

259
00:16:00,638 --> 00:16:03,398
is as much use to the
county as a glass hammer!

260
00:16:05,918 --> 00:16:09,878
I knew this would happen.
Typical. What's typical?

261
00:16:09,878 --> 00:16:13,798
That I'd wind up looking after
Mr Matthew. That's all I need.

262
00:16:13,798 --> 00:16:17,358
He hasn't thought it through.
I'm sorry to say, but he hasn't.

263
00:16:17,438 --> 00:16:19,878
Are you worried for your job, Mr Molesley?

264
00:16:19,878 --> 00:16:21,638
Me? Oh, heavens, no.

265
00:16:21,638 --> 00:16:25,038
No, no, no, I'm essential to
Mrs Crawley. She relies on me.

266
00:16:25,038 --> 00:16:27,798
That's what he said. Essential.

267
00:16:27,798 --> 00:16:31,078
Oh, yes, we're all essential.
Until we get sacked.

268
00:16:32,238 --> 00:16:36,078
How was it? Alfred was confused.

269
00:16:36,078 --> 00:16:39,358
He thought he'd been transported
to the Hotel Metropole.

270
00:16:40,158 --> 00:16:42,358
Cheer up. You'll get the hang of it.

271
00:16:42,358 --> 00:16:47,078
Will I? Oh, you're still here, Mr Molesley.

272
00:16:47,078 --> 00:16:50,198
I know. I only walked over
for a cup of tea and a chat.

273
00:16:50,198 --> 00:16:52,518
But I've outstayed my welcome. Nonsense.

274
00:16:52,518 --> 00:16:54,478
Why not have a bite with us?

275
00:16:54,478 --> 00:16:57,078
They won't be leaving
for a half hour or more.

276
00:16:57,078 --> 00:16:59,798
No, I'd better get back. I
wouldn't want them to get home

277
00:16:59,798 --> 00:17:02,638
and me not be there to let
them in. No, you wouldn't.

278
00:17:03,238 --> 00:17:05,238
Not when you're essential.

279
00:17:06,638 --> 00:17:08,598
Then why is he coming all the way here?

280
00:17:08,598 --> 00:17:11,678
Why not say it on the
telephone? I have no idea.

281
00:17:11,678 --> 00:17:14,358
If Mr Swire's lawyer wants
to see you and it's urgent,

282
00:17:14,358 --> 00:17:16,598
it means he's left you
something. I doubt it.

283
00:17:16,598 --> 00:17:18,478
I would have heard long before this.

284
00:17:18,478 --> 00:17:21,438
Anyway, I hope not. Why?

285
00:17:21,438 --> 00:17:23,638
Matthew. Do come on.

286
00:17:23,638 --> 00:17:25,958
The chauffeur's freezing
to death and so am I.

287
00:17:28,438 --> 00:17:30,598
Are you looking forward to the wedding?

288
00:17:30,598 --> 00:17:32,598
What do you think?

289
00:17:33,158 --> 00:17:35,358
I'm looking forward to all sorts of things.

290
00:17:35,758 --> 00:17:38,358
Don't make me blush. Matthew!

291
00:18:09,718 --> 00:18:11,718
Dearest Papa.

292
00:18:13,398 --> 00:18:16,158
Tell me, did you send the money?

293
00:18:16,438 --> 00:18:19,278
Please say yes. What money?

294
00:18:19,278 --> 00:18:21,278
Hello, Tom.

295
00:18:21,358 --> 00:18:23,358
Welcome to Downton.

296
00:18:23,518 --> 00:18:27,198
I hope I am welcome,
your ladyship. Of course.

297
00:18:28,078 --> 00:18:31,078
Alfred, would you take
the luggage for Mr Branson?

298
00:18:31,998 --> 00:18:33,798
There's tea in the library.

299
00:18:34,718 --> 00:18:36,718
Thank you.

300
00:18:45,238 --> 00:18:47,238
Hello, Mr Carson.

301
00:18:55,118 --> 00:18:58,918
Is that Mr Charkham I saw leaving? Yes.

302
00:18:58,918 --> 00:19:02,118
He said to make his apologies.
He was late for his train.

303
00:19:02,118 --> 00:19:04,118
What did he have to say for himself?

304
00:19:05,118 --> 00:19:07,118
I don't know where to start.

305
00:19:07,198 --> 00:19:12,478
Basically, it seems that Reggie Swire
did not wish to divide his fortune.

306
00:19:13,398 --> 00:19:17,158
So when Lavinia died, he made a
new will with a list of three heirs,

307
00:19:17,158 --> 00:19:19,478
of which I was the third.

308
00:19:20,398 --> 00:19:22,958
Why didn't the first name succeed?

309
00:19:22,958 --> 00:19:24,958
He died before Reggie.

310
00:19:25,158 --> 00:19:27,678
In the same epidemic that killed Lavinia.

311
00:19:28,118 --> 00:19:32,398
But at first they thought that the
second heir, a Mr Clive Pullbrook,

312
00:19:32,398 --> 00:19:34,398
would be easy to trace.

313
00:19:34,678 --> 00:19:36,678
How much money are we talking about?

314
00:19:37,438 --> 00:19:39,438
A lot.

315
00:19:39,718 --> 00:19:41,718
A huge amount. I had no idea.

316
00:19:41,998 --> 00:19:44,798
You could never have told
it from Reggie's way of life.

317
00:19:44,798 --> 00:19:46,798
Lucky Mr Pullbrook.

318
00:19:47,358 --> 00:19:49,358
This is it.

319
00:19:49,758 --> 00:19:52,278
Some time before Reggie's death,

320
00:19:52,278 --> 00:19:54,238
Pullbrook travelled to the East,

321
00:19:54,238 --> 00:19:57,078
to India, to some tea
plantations he owned there.

322
00:19:58,758 --> 00:20:01,918
And? He's never been heard of since.

323
00:20:01,918 --> 00:20:05,958
They've made enquiries. They've sent
an agent out to visit his property.

324
00:20:06,398 --> 00:20:08,398
There's no sign of him.

325
00:20:09,878 --> 00:20:12,558
I'm sorry. I won't. And that's flat.

326
00:20:12,998 --> 00:20:14,998
Then you'll have to do it, Mr Carson.

327
00:20:14,998 --> 00:20:17,118
I'm not dressing a chauffeur.

328
00:20:17,118 --> 00:20:19,078
He is not a chauffeur now.

329
00:20:19,078 --> 00:20:21,038
Anyway, you don't have to dress him,

330
00:20:21,038 --> 00:20:23,318
just see he's got everything he needs.

331
00:20:23,318 --> 00:20:27,518
I'm not often as one
with Mr Barrow, but no.

332
00:20:27,518 --> 00:20:29,958
Then Alfred must do it. Alfred?

333
00:20:29,958 --> 00:20:33,398
He wouldn't know what to do
beyond collecting dirty shoes.

334
00:20:33,398 --> 00:20:35,398
Well, he'll have to learn.

335
00:20:46,014 --> 00:20:48,494
Is it an Irish tradition?

336
00:20:48,494 --> 00:20:50,734
What? She means not changing.

337
00:20:50,734 --> 00:20:53,654
Of course it isn't,
Granny. It might have been.

338
00:20:53,654 --> 00:20:56,494
You don't change on the
first night of a voyage.

339
00:20:56,494 --> 00:20:58,854
No, my lady, I don't own a set of tails

340
00:20:58,854 --> 00:21:02,174
or a dinner jacket either. I
wouldn't get any use out of them.

341
00:21:02,174 --> 00:21:05,934
Well, I hope you own a morning coat
since you're here for a wedding.

342
00:21:05,934 --> 00:21:10,054
No, I'm afraid I don't. We live
a different kind of life, Papa.

343
00:21:10,054 --> 00:21:12,054
Obviously.

344
00:21:12,294 --> 00:21:14,694
Could you lower it a
bit, please, Mr Carson?

345
00:21:16,694 --> 00:21:19,334
You should buy a Downton
wardrobe and leave it here.

346
00:21:19,334 --> 00:21:22,334
Then you won't have to pack
when you come. What a good idea.

347
00:21:22,334 --> 00:21:25,694
I'm afraid I can't turn into
somebody else just to please you.

348
00:21:25,694 --> 00:21:29,614
More's the pity. Oh, no, why
should you change to please us?

349
00:21:29,614 --> 00:21:31,734
What is the general feeling in Ireland now?

350
00:21:31,734 --> 00:21:36,294
That we're in sight of throwing off the
English yoke. Do you approve of the new act?

351
00:21:36,294 --> 00:21:39,254
Would you of your country being
divided by a foreign power?

352
00:21:39,254 --> 00:21:42,294
Well, won't it bring home rule
for Southern Ireland nearer?

353
00:21:42,294 --> 00:21:45,214
Home rule on English terms
presided over by an English king.

354
00:21:45,214 --> 00:21:47,054
Is keeping the monarchy a problem?

355
00:21:47,054 --> 00:21:50,374
Would it be a problem for you
to be ruled by the German Kaiser?

356
00:21:52,734 --> 00:21:54,774
Carson, are you all right?

357
00:21:54,774 --> 00:21:57,654
I've been very clumsy,
my lord. I do apologise.

358
00:21:57,654 --> 00:22:02,254
Is it true that Irish gardens
have more variety than ours?

359
00:22:02,254 --> 00:22:06,534
Oh, yes. Don't you remember Lady
Dufferin's ball at Claneboye?

360
00:22:06,534 --> 00:22:08,494
The gardens there were heavenly.

361
00:22:11,054 --> 00:22:14,494
I thought them very down on him.
That is because you know nothing.

362
00:22:14,494 --> 00:22:16,414
And wasn't he down on them,

363
00:22:16,414 --> 00:22:19,294
insulting our country, insulting the King?

364
00:22:19,294 --> 00:22:22,134
I thought it was a miracle
his lordship held his temper.

365
00:22:22,134 --> 00:22:24,494
But it must be hard, Mr Carson.

366
00:22:24,494 --> 00:22:28,374
To sit up there with people
he used to drive around.

367
00:22:29,214 --> 00:22:31,214
It is hard, Mrs Hughes.

368
00:22:32,534 --> 00:22:34,574
Please, sit down.

369
00:22:34,574 --> 00:22:36,574
Is there something we can do, sir?

370
00:22:37,974 --> 00:22:40,134
I just wanted to come down to say hello.

371
00:22:40,134 --> 00:22:44,734
I wouldn't want you to think I'd got
too big for my boots. That's nice.

372
00:22:45,654 --> 00:22:47,694
I hope you and Lady Sybil are well?

373
00:22:47,694 --> 00:22:49,694
We are. Thank you.

374
00:22:49,694 --> 00:22:52,094
And we've been following
the story of Mr Bates.

375
00:22:52,094 --> 00:22:53,854
Mary keeps us informed.

376
00:22:56,254 --> 00:22:58,974
Still, I mustn't interrupt your dinner.

377
00:22:59,374 --> 00:23:01,374
Thank you for coming down.

378
00:23:07,214 --> 00:23:09,054
He's settled into his new life.

379
00:23:09,054 --> 00:23:11,334
'Mary keeps us informed.'

380
00:23:11,334 --> 00:23:14,814
Well, he knows her now.
What's that got to do with it?

381
00:23:14,814 --> 00:23:18,494
His lordship would never call her
Mary when talking to me. Never.

382
00:23:19,734 --> 00:23:23,214
If he wants to play their game,
he'd better learn their rules.

383
00:23:24,854 --> 00:23:27,974
Tomorrow let's ask the servants
to come up and dine with us

384
00:23:27,974 --> 00:23:29,934
to make things easier.

385
00:23:29,934 --> 00:23:33,574
You must get him to stop calling
Granny 'my lady'. And Mama.

386
00:23:33,574 --> 00:23:37,374
We need something that doesn't
sound too stiff and grand.

387
00:23:37,374 --> 00:23:39,374
Lady Grantham, of course.

388
00:23:39,854 --> 00:23:41,854
And he can call me Lord Grantham.

389
00:23:43,054 --> 00:23:45,534
That doesn't sound stiff or grand at all.

390
00:23:45,894 --> 00:23:47,894
One step at a time.

391
00:23:52,654 --> 00:23:55,574
So what did the lawyer
want? I presume he turned up.

392
00:23:55,574 --> 00:23:58,294
He did, and it's rather complicated.

393
00:23:58,854 --> 00:24:01,454
But you were right. It
was about Reggie's will.

394
00:24:01,534 --> 00:24:03,654
So he's left you something?

395
00:24:03,654 --> 00:24:06,934
Never mind that now. Sit down
and tell me about the relations

396
00:24:06,934 --> 00:24:10,734
that are coming for the wedding. I
want to unscramble them in my head.

397
00:24:11,054 --> 00:24:14,734
Go to bed when you're done.
I'll go to bed when I'm ready.

398
00:24:15,694 --> 00:24:19,374
What's happened to you? Have you
swapped places with your evil twin?

399
00:24:19,374 --> 00:24:22,814
I'd like to know where the new
maid is. That's what you promised.

400
00:24:22,814 --> 00:24:24,814
They've got a footman. Where's the maid?

401
00:24:24,814 --> 00:24:27,974
I know and I'm sorry, but
I spoke to Mr Carson tonight

402
00:24:27,974 --> 00:24:30,254
and they won't be taking anyone new on.

403
00:24:30,254 --> 00:24:34,254
Except a footman. I don't
know how Mr Carson managed it

404
00:24:34,254 --> 00:24:36,494
because his lordship's put his foot down.

405
00:24:36,494 --> 00:24:40,614
But you're called my assistant now and
you've seven shillings extra every month.

406
00:24:40,614 --> 00:24:43,694
You've still kept me here with
a dishonest representation.

407
00:24:43,694 --> 00:24:46,654
Oh, dear! Have you swallowed a dictionary?

408
00:24:49,894 --> 00:24:53,014
Somehow none of it seems to
matter when we're in Dublin.

409
00:24:53,454 --> 00:24:55,774
Class and all that just seems to fade away.

410
00:24:56,694 --> 00:25:00,574
I'm Mrs Branson and we get on with
our lives like millions of others.

411
00:25:01,254 --> 00:25:03,254
But here he feels so patronised.

412
00:25:04,014 --> 00:25:07,414
And he hates it. But you don't regret it?

413
00:25:07,414 --> 00:25:09,414
No, never.

414
00:25:10,254 --> 00:25:12,254
He's a wonderful, wonderful man.

415
00:25:12,814 --> 00:25:14,654
I just wish you knew him.

416
00:25:14,654 --> 00:25:16,534
Darling, we will know him.

417
00:25:16,534 --> 00:25:18,734
We'll know him and value him. I promise.

418
00:25:20,454 --> 00:25:22,454
Anyway... 

419
00:25:23,334 --> 00:25:27,094
I'd best go upstairs and make
sure he's not too suicidal.

420
00:25:27,454 --> 00:25:29,454
Good night.

421
00:25:29,734 --> 00:25:32,534
Oh, by the way, I don't
know if Mama's told you... 

422
00:25:32,534 --> 00:25:35,454
But the whole Grey family's
coming tomorrow night.

423
00:25:36,694 --> 00:25:38,694
Including Larry?

424
00:25:39,334 --> 00:25:41,334
Crikey. You'd better warn Tom.

425
00:25:42,334 --> 00:25:46,534
Oh, and, Sybil, I wouldn't tell
Papa about being Mrs Branson.

426
00:25:52,134 --> 00:25:53,974
But who are the Greys?

427
00:25:53,974 --> 00:25:56,134
And why does it matter that they're coming?

428
00:25:56,134 --> 00:25:58,494
The father, Lord Merton,
is Mary's Godfather.

429
00:25:58,494 --> 00:26:01,814
But Larry Grey used to be
keen on me, when we were young.

430
00:26:01,814 --> 00:26:03,854
Were you keen on him?

431
00:26:03,854 --> 00:26:05,854
No. I don't think so.

432
00:26:06,654 --> 00:26:08,654
I can hardly remember, to be honest.

433
00:26:08,934 --> 00:26:12,934
So what are you saying?
Nothing particularly.

434
00:26:13,654 --> 00:26:16,734
But we could run into
Ripon and find some tails.

435
00:26:16,734 --> 00:26:18,734
We have the money.

436
00:26:18,734 --> 00:26:20,734
I won't spend more of that money.

437
00:26:21,854 --> 00:26:23,854
All right.

438
00:26:24,694 --> 00:26:27,214
Please don't talk about
Ireland all the time.

439
00:26:27,654 --> 00:26:29,934
I just want to make things easier for you.

440
00:26:31,534 --> 00:26:33,534
For me or for you?

441
00:26:33,894 --> 00:26:35,894
Don't disappoint me, Sybil.

442
00:26:36,214 --> 00:26:38,214
Not now that we're here.

443
00:26:47,614 --> 00:26:49,494
Shall I order the car?

444
00:26:49,494 --> 00:26:51,614
I don't think I can
refuse a lift with Mother

445
00:26:51,614 --> 00:26:53,894
and then make the poor man go out again.

446
00:26:53,894 --> 00:26:55,894
I'll walk. It might rain.

447
00:26:56,534 --> 00:26:58,334
Then I'll get wet.

448
00:26:59,654 --> 00:27:01,654
Now come and kiss me.

449
00:27:05,374 --> 00:27:09,734
So, if they can't find Mr...Pillbox,
what will you do with the money?

450
00:27:09,734 --> 00:27:12,494
Pullbrook. And they will find him.

451
00:27:13,294 --> 00:27:15,494
But if they don't?

452
00:27:16,134 --> 00:27:18,334
Then I'll decide what to do.

453
00:27:19,054 --> 00:27:21,054
Or we will.

454
00:27:23,974 --> 00:27:27,814
Because I can't keep it. No, of course not.

455
00:27:31,334 --> 00:27:35,254
Why have you so heavily invested in
one enterprise? Wasn't it foolish?

456
00:27:35,654 --> 00:27:38,494
Has some of my fortune been lost?

457
00:27:39,974 --> 00:27:41,974
Some.

458
00:27:43,494 --> 00:27:45,414
All.

459
00:27:45,414 --> 00:27:47,414
Almost all.

460
00:27:55,134 --> 00:27:57,134
Oh, my dear.

461
00:28:02,854 --> 00:28:04,854
How terrible for you.

462
00:28:05,694 --> 00:28:07,814
It's not so good for you.

463
00:28:07,814 --> 00:28:11,294
Don't worry about me. I'm an
American. Have gun, will travel.

464
00:28:13,654 --> 00:28:16,014
Thank God for you, anyway.

465
00:28:20,134 --> 00:28:22,134
You know what?

466
00:28:22,814 --> 00:28:25,014
I'm glad we have a wedding to celebrate.

467
00:28:26,814 --> 00:28:28,814
Let's make sure it's a great day.

468
00:28:32,294 --> 00:28:35,294
If it's to be our last, let's
make it a wonderful last.

469
00:28:37,574 --> 00:28:41,294
Enjoy our lovely home and the lovely
people we spend our life among.

470
00:28:55,134 --> 00:28:57,214
Bit early for drowning your sorrows.

471
00:28:57,214 --> 00:29:00,134
I thought it might be better
if I moved down to the pub.

472
00:29:00,774 --> 00:29:04,814
You're not serious? I can't go through
many more dinners like last night.

473
00:29:04,814 --> 00:29:06,814
You don't make it easy for them.

474
00:29:07,174 --> 00:29:10,694
Do you really think you can
recruit cousin Robert for Sinn Fein?

475
00:29:10,814 --> 00:29:12,854
I don't know what gets into me.

476
00:29:12,854 --> 00:29:15,734
I can see them staring and I
know they don't want me here.

477
00:29:15,734 --> 00:29:17,574
Well, don't include me. Or Mary.

478
00:29:17,574 --> 00:29:21,174
She wasn't too keen on the idea of
a chauffeur for a brother-in-law.

479
00:29:21,174 --> 00:29:23,294
Forget that. She's a pragmatist.

480
00:29:23,534 --> 00:29:26,894
She could be a tough fighter,
too. Let's hope she's not tested.

481
00:29:26,894 --> 00:29:31,174
Forget this and walk back. We're
brothers-in-law with high-minded wives.

482
00:29:31,174 --> 00:29:33,174
We'd better stick together.

483
00:29:40,214 --> 00:29:42,254
It's all there.

484
00:29:42,254 --> 00:29:44,974
Friends - though there
weren't too many -

485
00:29:44,974 --> 00:29:47,054
tradesmen, acquaintances.

486
00:29:47,054 --> 00:29:49,574
But I can't see what
you'll get out of them.

487
00:29:49,574 --> 00:29:52,254
I do not believe, when Vera
decided to kill herself,

488
00:29:52,254 --> 00:29:54,814
she never mentioned it
to another living soul.

489
00:29:54,814 --> 00:29:56,574
We know she left no note.

490
00:29:56,574 --> 00:29:58,574
I wish to God she had.

491
00:29:59,214 --> 00:30:02,254
But why are you sure it
was suicide and not murder?

492
00:30:02,254 --> 00:30:04,414
Well, I know you didn't kill her.

493
00:30:04,414 --> 00:30:06,654
What's the alternative?

494
00:30:06,654 --> 00:30:10,414
A thief broke in, cooked an arsenic
pie and forced her to eat it?

495
00:30:10,814 --> 00:30:13,094
It's not a very likely scenario.

496
00:30:14,174 --> 00:30:16,174
You can see why they convicted me.

497
00:30:16,574 --> 00:30:18,934
I'm going to write to everyone in the book,

498
00:30:18,934 --> 00:30:22,294
in case she said, or,
please God, wrote anything

499
00:30:22,294 --> 00:30:24,294
that could suggest a desire to die.

500
00:30:24,974 --> 00:30:27,414
How long will that take?

501
00:30:27,414 --> 00:30:30,774
Why? Are you going somewhere?

502
00:30:33,454 --> 00:30:37,334
I should have gone into cooking.
I could pick it up in a trice.

503
00:30:37,334 --> 00:30:40,254
Why didn't you, then? It's
a hard ladder for a man.

504
00:30:40,734 --> 00:30:44,374
For every Escoffier and Monsieur
Careme there's a thousand dogsbodies

505
00:30:44,374 --> 00:30:47,094
taking orders from a
cross, red-faced old woman.

506
00:30:47,094 --> 00:30:48,854
Who's this you're discussing?

507
00:30:48,854 --> 00:30:51,854
Hello, Mrs Patmore. I didn't
see you standing there.

508
00:30:51,854 --> 00:30:53,854
Obviously not.

509
00:30:54,254 --> 00:30:58,334
Sarah thought I'd be better off as a
butler and that's what I'm trying for.

510
00:30:58,334 --> 00:31:01,094
I think you're right. I'd
rather be giving the orders.

511
00:31:01,094 --> 00:31:04,774
To a cross and red-faced
old woman. Yes, we know.

512
00:31:09,174 --> 00:31:11,654
There. Is that what you meant?

513
00:31:11,654 --> 00:31:13,654
Yes. Perfect.

514
00:31:14,374 --> 00:31:16,374
Slightly new, but not too different.

515
00:31:17,934 --> 00:31:20,134
We'll see if Sir Anthony notices.

516
00:31:23,254 --> 00:31:26,374
I know they all think he's
too old for me, but he's not.

517
00:31:26,574 --> 00:31:29,454
Bates is older than you and
you're as happy as lovebirds.

518
00:31:30,214 --> 00:31:32,214
Our situation is hardly ideal,

519
00:31:33,534 --> 00:31:36,054
but, yes, we're very happy together.

520
00:31:36,894 --> 00:31:38,894
Which is all that matters.

521
00:31:39,414 --> 00:31:41,414
As I keep telling them.

522
00:31:46,334 --> 00:31:49,894
I've no time to talk. His
lordship's not come up yet.

523
00:31:50,334 --> 00:31:52,334
Well? What is it?

524
00:31:53,134 --> 00:31:57,014
I was hoping you could help young
Alfred to find his way about.

525
00:31:58,254 --> 00:32:00,854
As a footman, you mean. As a valet.

526
00:32:02,134 --> 00:32:04,174
He's looking after Mr Branson now.

527
00:32:04,174 --> 00:32:07,054
Though I daresay a
chauffeur can dress himself.

528
00:32:07,054 --> 00:32:10,374
But you could tell him what he
needs to know, give him an advantage.

529
00:32:10,374 --> 00:32:12,214
Why? What's the rush?

530
00:32:12,214 --> 00:32:15,134
You've heard Mr Matthew
has turned down Mr Molesley?

531
00:32:15,654 --> 00:32:17,654
Blimey, you don't want much, do you?

532
00:32:18,974 --> 00:32:21,614
Can you remember what I had
to go through to be a valet?

533
00:32:21,614 --> 00:32:24,014
Of course. I watched it, didn't I?

534
00:32:24,014 --> 00:32:27,014
But young Alfred is to make
the great leap in one bound?

535
00:32:27,014 --> 00:32:30,134
Well, I'm sorry, Miss
O'Brien, but I'm not convinced.

536
00:32:31,174 --> 00:32:33,174
If you'll excuse me.

537
00:33:03,463 --> 00:33:05,783
It's infuriating.
There's nothing he can do.

538
00:33:05,783 --> 00:33:07,823
I don't agree. I think it's feeble.

539
00:33:07,823 --> 00:33:11,823
He should will himself not to be
ill and then collapse the next day.

540
00:33:11,823 --> 00:33:15,503
Whom will you ask instead? I'm not sure.

541
00:33:15,903 --> 00:33:17,743
I've known Sybil all my life.

542
00:33:17,743 --> 00:33:21,543
You can imagine how curious I was
when I found out you'd be here tonight.

543
00:33:21,543 --> 00:33:23,903
Never thought we'd meet in person.

544
00:33:23,903 --> 00:33:26,303
As opposed to what? In spirit?

545
00:33:28,343 --> 00:33:31,623
Well, you see, to us, in marrying you

546
00:33:31,623 --> 00:33:34,663
it seemed like Sybil had
left Downton Abbey forever.

547
00:33:36,183 --> 00:33:38,183
If you know what I mean.

548
00:33:38,863 --> 00:33:40,863
I know exactly what you mean.

549
00:33:42,623 --> 00:33:46,943
Did they lose your suitcase on the
way over? How maddening for you.

550
00:33:46,943 --> 00:33:49,743
No, my suitcase arrived safely, thank you.

551
00:33:49,743 --> 00:33:51,743
Along with my manners.

552
00:33:56,903 --> 00:34:00,103
He's still dressed as the man
from the Prudential, I see.

553
00:34:00,983 --> 00:34:04,303
Yes, it's nice to have someone
from the real world, isn't it?

554
00:34:07,663 --> 00:34:10,583
Hello, Mama. Can I tempt you
to one of these new cocktails?

555
00:34:10,583 --> 00:34:12,543
I don't think so.

556
00:34:12,543 --> 00:34:15,383
They look too exciting for
so early in the evening.

557
00:34:15,383 --> 00:34:17,503
Don't you think so, Carson?

558
00:34:17,503 --> 00:34:20,023
Better avoided, my lady. I think so.

559
00:34:21,463 --> 00:34:24,863
What a pleasure it is to see
you out and about, Sir Anthony.

560
00:34:25,383 --> 00:34:29,263
I want to say, 'Can I be of any
help?' but you don't seem to need any.

561
00:34:29,263 --> 00:34:32,783
He doesn't need help at all, do
you? He won't let me do anything.

562
00:34:32,783 --> 00:34:34,583
Mustn't be a nuisance, you know.

563
00:34:35,303 --> 00:34:37,783
Are you coming to the wedding? Of course.

564
00:34:39,223 --> 00:34:41,223
Well, if you really want me.

565
00:34:41,863 --> 00:34:44,183
I do. I really do.

566
00:34:45,863 --> 00:34:49,783
You look very nice. Have you done
something jolly with your hair?

567
00:34:51,103 --> 00:34:52,943
I say. What the devil... 

568
00:34:52,943 --> 00:34:54,503
What is it?

569
00:34:54,503 --> 00:34:56,503
Dinner is served, your ladyship.

570
00:35:00,223 --> 00:35:02,023
How's it going? Awkward.

571
00:35:02,023 --> 00:35:05,663
Mr Branson's well away and
Lady Sybil doesn't like it much.

572
00:35:05,663 --> 00:35:07,783
He's only had one cocktail.

573
00:35:07,783 --> 00:35:11,183
Maybe he was drinking before
he came down to calm his nerves.

574
00:35:13,543 --> 00:35:16,423
No, I don't agree, and I
don't care who knows it.

575
00:35:16,423 --> 00:35:19,303
The Black and Tans are there
to restore order, are they?

576
00:35:19,303 --> 00:35:21,703
Why don't they just murder
the entire population

577
00:35:21,703 --> 00:35:23,903
and then you wouldn't
hear a squeak from them.

578
00:35:23,903 --> 00:35:27,823
Is there any way to shut him up? If I
knew that, he wouldn't be here at all.

579
00:35:27,823 --> 00:35:30,343
Are you interested in
Irish politics, Lord Merton?

580
00:35:30,343 --> 00:35:33,423
I was only just saying... He's
interested in Irish repression.

581
00:35:33,423 --> 00:35:37,223
Like all of you. Look, of
course this stuff matters to you.

582
00:35:37,223 --> 00:35:39,063
Yes, it does matter... 

583
00:35:39,063 --> 00:35:42,663
..this stuff. It matters a very great deal.

584
00:35:42,663 --> 00:35:44,903
What's so funny? Nothing.

585
00:35:44,903 --> 00:35:48,103
I'm just enjoying this vivid
display of Irish character.

586
00:35:49,143 --> 00:35:52,063
Please, Tom. We don't
need to wear everyone out.

587
00:35:52,303 --> 00:35:57,983
Why? What's the matter? Am
I not being polite enough?

588
00:35:58,623 --> 00:36:01,223
What a minute. This was
down to you, wasn't it?

589
00:36:01,223 --> 00:36:03,583
I don't know what you mean. Yes, you do.

590
00:36:03,583 --> 00:36:07,503
You put something in his drink,
didn't you? Just before we came in.

591
00:36:08,383 --> 00:36:11,503
That's not true, is it, Larry?
What a beastly thing to do.

592
00:36:11,503 --> 00:36:15,023
Oh, come on, Edith. That's not like
you. You could always take a joke.

593
00:36:15,023 --> 00:36:17,023
A bully's defence.

594
00:36:17,023 --> 00:36:21,543
Listen, Mr Grey has given Mr Branson
something to make him appear drunk.

595
00:36:21,543 --> 00:36:24,783
Could it be drink? No, not
drink. Some horrible pill.

596
00:36:24,783 --> 00:36:27,583
Sybil, take him upstairs.
Il ne manque que ca.

597
00:36:28,223 --> 00:36:30,303
Tom has been the victim of a cruel prank

598
00:36:30,303 --> 00:36:33,263
which I know you'll all
be kind enough to forget.

599
00:36:33,263 --> 00:36:37,863
Forgive, perhaps. Forget?
Never. Is this true, Larry?

600
00:36:37,863 --> 00:36:40,983
I don't know why you're
getting so hot under the collar.

601
00:36:40,983 --> 00:36:42,943
He's only a grubby little chauffeur.

602
00:36:42,943 --> 00:36:44,903
Be silent this instant, sir!

603
00:36:47,623 --> 00:36:50,463
I apologise for my son,
Mr Branson. Unreservedly.

604
00:36:51,543 --> 00:36:54,143
I only hope you'll
recover before the wedding.

605
00:36:55,263 --> 00:36:57,183
I hope so too.

606
00:36:57,183 --> 00:36:59,583
Since I want him to be my best man.

607
00:37:03,223 --> 00:37:05,863
Bravo. Well said.

608
00:37:05,863 --> 00:37:07,983
Do you really mean it? Honestly?

609
00:37:07,983 --> 00:37:11,183
I've told you. If we're mad enough
to take on the Crawley girls,

610
00:37:11,183 --> 00:37:13,183
we have to stick together.

611
00:37:13,743 --> 00:37:15,863
Thank you, Matthew. Thank you so much.

612
00:37:18,863 --> 00:37:22,583
That was rather marvellous of you
to expose Larry Grey like that.

613
00:37:22,583 --> 00:37:25,983
You saved the day. I wouldn't
say that. Matthew saved the day.

614
00:37:25,983 --> 00:37:27,983
No, it was you.

615
00:37:28,943 --> 00:37:32,663
I do hope we'll see a bit more
of you once the wedding's over.

616
00:37:32,663 --> 00:37:34,743
Well... Wouldn't you like that?

617
00:37:34,743 --> 00:37:36,743
I should like that very much.

618
00:37:37,543 --> 00:37:39,463
Much more than I probably ought to.

619
00:37:40,103 --> 00:37:42,223
Edith, let Sir Anthony go.

620
00:37:51,463 --> 00:37:53,463
Goodnight, Papa.

621
00:38:00,983 --> 00:38:02,943
Well, that's the last of them.

622
00:38:02,943 --> 00:38:05,263
Where are the others? They've gone to bed.

623
00:38:05,263 --> 00:38:07,263
So's Edith. And so should we.

624
00:38:08,783 --> 00:38:12,503
Golly. What a night for
the county to feed off.

625
00:38:13,063 --> 00:38:15,863
It was good of Matthew to show solidarity.

626
00:38:16,343 --> 00:38:18,343
I suppose so.

627
00:38:18,783 --> 00:38:21,543
We're going to need all the
solidarity we can muster.

628
00:38:21,543 --> 00:38:23,543
When will you tell the girls?

629
00:38:26,223 --> 00:38:29,903
I think I should tell Mary now.
Not before the wedding, surely.

630
00:38:30,343 --> 00:38:33,463
I must. They're disagreeing
about where they should live.

631
00:38:33,463 --> 00:38:35,783
It would be wrong for
me to keep it from them.

632
00:38:35,783 --> 00:38:39,063
They can discuss it on the
honeymoon and decide more sensibly.

633
00:38:40,583 --> 00:38:45,223
Should we say something to your
mother when she gets here? No.

634
00:38:45,223 --> 00:38:49,343
She'll go into state mourning and
cast a pall over the whole proceedings.

635
00:38:50,023 --> 00:38:53,503
Thank God she missed tonight's drama
or we'd never hear the end of it.

636
00:38:53,503 --> 00:38:57,103
Don't worry. She'll bring
enough drama of her own.

637
00:38:57,783 --> 00:38:59,783
Hm.

638
00:39:03,583 --> 00:39:05,623
It won't work, you know.

639
00:39:05,623 --> 00:39:09,143
If you don't admit your
guilt, they won't let you go.

640
00:39:09,143 --> 00:39:11,303
How can I admit what isn't true?

641
00:39:12,463 --> 00:39:15,503
Why do you have to be so pious?

642
00:39:17,863 --> 00:39:19,863
You're a touchy fella, aren't you?

643
00:39:21,623 --> 00:39:24,143
Don't push me, Craig.

644
00:39:24,143 --> 00:39:26,143
Is that a warning?

645
00:39:26,503 --> 00:39:29,143
Yes. Yes, it is.

646
00:39:32,223 --> 00:39:34,223
I'm warning you.

647
00:39:41,583 --> 00:39:43,663
I'm not sure about the hat.

648
00:39:43,663 --> 00:39:45,823
Is it supposed to look crooked?

649
00:39:45,823 --> 00:39:49,423
Don't listen to her. I love it.
You're not to change a thing.

650
00:39:49,423 --> 00:39:52,223
Anna? I think you look lovely, my lady.

651
00:39:54,023 --> 00:39:56,663
Stop. Wait. Who is it?

652
00:39:56,663 --> 00:39:59,943
Your long-suffering papa.
I suppose he can come in.

653
00:40:02,623 --> 00:40:04,983
What's this for? Going away.

654
00:40:05,343 --> 00:40:07,903
How does it look? Expensive.

655
00:40:08,583 --> 00:40:10,583
Twice the national debt, I'm afraid.

656
00:40:10,583 --> 00:40:12,863
But I know you don't mind.

657
00:40:13,863 --> 00:40:16,823
Can I have one moment alone
to give Mary my blessing?

658
00:40:16,823 --> 00:40:20,423
That's lovely. Shoo, everyone.

659
00:40:26,423 --> 00:40:28,423
Go on, bless me.

660
00:40:29,143 --> 00:40:33,703
Of course. But there's something
I feel I ought to tell you first.

661
00:40:34,543 --> 00:40:38,303
I wanted to wait until you got
back, but I don't believe I can.

662
00:40:39,143 --> 00:40:41,343
That sounds rather ominous.

663
00:40:46,343 --> 00:40:49,183
Hello, Mr Molesley. I got a
message to call on Mrs Crawley.

664
00:40:49,183 --> 00:40:53,503
Very good, sir. If you'd like to
give me your hat and coat, sir.

665
00:40:53,983 --> 00:40:57,823
Are you going up to the house
to welcome the Queen of Sheba?

666
00:40:57,823 --> 00:40:59,823
Oh, I think so.

667
00:40:59,823 --> 00:41:01,863
Are you? No.

668
00:41:01,863 --> 00:41:03,823
I'll pay homage at dinner.

669
00:41:03,823 --> 00:41:06,863
I've always admired the way
Mrs Levinson is never overawed

670
00:41:06,863 --> 00:41:12,223
by the set-up at Downton. Was
Napoleon overawed by the Bourbons?

671
00:41:13,663 --> 00:41:15,663
Come in, Tom.

672
00:41:17,263 --> 00:41:19,783
May I call you Tom? Of course.

673
00:41:20,783 --> 00:41:23,583
Good afternoon, my lady.
That is, Lady Grantham.

674
00:41:23,943 --> 00:41:25,943
I'm glad to find you here because... 

675
00:41:26,863 --> 00:41:28,943
I want to apologise for last night.

676
00:41:29,543 --> 00:41:32,423
Oh, there's no need. We
know it was not your fault.

677
00:41:32,423 --> 00:41:36,663
You weren't the first drunk in
that dining room, I can assure you.

678
00:41:37,303 --> 00:41:39,343
Only the first republican.

679
00:41:39,343 --> 00:41:41,103
Well, you've got me there.

680
00:41:42,183 --> 00:41:44,223
Why was it you wanted to see me?

681
00:41:44,223 --> 00:41:47,023
We asked Molesley to look out
Matthew's old morning coat.

682
00:41:47,023 --> 00:41:49,063
He's confident he can make it fit.

683
00:41:49,063 --> 00:41:50,903
That's very kind, ladies.

684
00:41:50,903 --> 00:41:53,503
But I don't approve of these costumes.

685
00:41:53,503 --> 00:41:55,623
I see them as the uniform of oppression

686
00:41:55,623 --> 00:41:57,863
and I should be uncomfortable wearing them.

687
00:41:57,863 --> 00:41:59,863
Have you quite finished?

688
00:42:00,623 --> 00:42:02,623
I have. Good.

689
00:42:03,383 --> 00:42:05,583
Please take off your coat.

690
00:42:06,743 --> 00:42:08,583
Molesley, do help him.

691
00:42:08,583 --> 00:42:10,583
If you'll just slip it off, sir.

692
00:42:15,383 --> 00:42:17,983
Shouldn't he put the waistcoat on first?

693
00:42:17,983 --> 00:42:21,703
What's going on? They're
forcing me into a morning coat.

694
00:42:21,703 --> 00:42:25,903
He has no say in it? No,
he doesn't. And nor do you.

695
00:42:26,583 --> 00:42:29,063
Well, what do you think, Molesley?

696
00:42:29,063 --> 00:42:32,023
It'll need lifting a little here, my lady.

697
00:42:32,383 --> 00:42:34,263
I'll move the buttons. Fine.

698
00:42:34,263 --> 00:42:36,903
I think the shoulders look odd.

699
00:42:36,903 --> 00:42:38,903
Mm.

700
00:43:02,703 --> 00:43:05,543
Come war and peace, Downton still stands

701
00:43:05,543 --> 00:43:07,543
and the Crawleys are still in it.

702
00:43:09,023 --> 00:43:11,103
Cora. Mother, how lovely to see you.

703
00:43:11,103 --> 00:43:13,103
As long as it is.

704
00:43:13,103 --> 00:43:16,383
Robert, aren't you going to kiss
me? With the greatest enthusiasm.

705
00:43:17,703 --> 00:43:19,623
Tell me, where does this come from?

706
00:43:20,183 --> 00:43:22,183
I hired it in Liverpool. Why?

707
00:43:22,183 --> 00:43:24,943
I thought it might be a
gift from the US government

708
00:43:24,943 --> 00:43:26,983
to help get Britain back on its feet.

709
00:43:27,863 --> 00:43:32,223
Carson and Mrs Hughes. The world
has moved on since last we met.

710
00:43:32,223 --> 00:43:34,743
And we have moved on with it, madam.

711
00:43:34,743 --> 00:43:36,583
Really?

712
00:43:36,583 --> 00:43:39,983
It seems so strange to think
of the English embracing change.

713
00:43:39,983 --> 00:43:42,063
Mrs Hughes, this is my maid... Reed.

714
00:43:42,463 --> 00:43:45,703
Sybil, tell me all about the
arrangements for the birth.

715
00:43:45,703 --> 00:43:48,823
We do these things so
much better in the States.

716
00:43:49,303 --> 00:43:51,743
Edith, still no-one special?

717
00:43:52,383 --> 00:43:56,863
Oh, well, never mind. You must take
a tip from the modern American girl.

718
00:43:56,863 --> 00:43:59,503
Mary, dearest Mary.

719
00:43:59,503 --> 00:44:02,743
Now, you tell me all of your wedding plans

720
00:44:02,743 --> 00:44:05,023
and I'll see what I can do to improve them.

721
00:44:15,703 --> 00:44:20,063
What's the matter with
you? Mrs Patmore knows.

722
00:44:20,063 --> 00:44:23,303
Shall I tell you Mrs Levinson's
requirements during her stay?

723
00:44:23,303 --> 00:44:27,063
No, tell her. Er, yes,
Miss Reed. How can I help?

724
00:44:27,383 --> 00:44:30,303
To start with I will need
goats' milk in the mornings.

725
00:44:30,303 --> 00:44:32,303
Goats' milk? Fancy that.

726
00:44:32,503 --> 00:44:34,303
She drinks only boiled water.

727
00:44:34,303 --> 00:44:36,663
Really? In England, that is.

728
00:44:36,663 --> 00:44:39,303
Shouldn't Daisy do this?
I ought to take the tea up.

729
00:44:39,303 --> 00:44:41,303
I'll have it ready in a moment.

730
00:44:44,063 --> 00:44:47,423
No fats, no crab and nothing
from the marrow family.

731
00:44:48,303 --> 00:44:50,983
Do explain again how exactly

732
00:44:50,983 --> 00:44:53,503
you are related to all of us, Mr Crawley.

733
00:44:54,543 --> 00:44:56,703
Rather distantly, I'm afraid.

734
00:44:56,703 --> 00:45:01,503
My great-great-grandfather was
a younger son of a third earl.

735
00:45:01,503 --> 00:45:05,903
My. I'm going to have to write
that down, so I can study it.

736
00:45:05,903 --> 00:45:09,103
Look at our page in Burke's.
You'll find Matthew there.

737
00:45:09,103 --> 00:45:11,343
Good. Because I would so like to understand

738
00:45:11,343 --> 00:45:14,543
why he gets to inherit
my late husband's money.

739
00:45:15,223 --> 00:45:17,423
I know. It's funny, isn't it?

740
00:45:18,183 --> 00:45:20,343
Not everyone shares your sense of humour.

741
00:45:20,343 --> 00:45:23,263
Surely it doesn't matter now
that they're getting married.

742
00:45:23,263 --> 00:45:26,583
In fact, we'd better turn him out
or it'll be bad luck for tomorrow.

743
00:45:26,583 --> 00:45:28,583
CORA: Quite right.

744
00:45:35,143 --> 00:45:37,863
You must be the chauffeur
I've heard so much about.

745
00:45:37,863 --> 00:45:41,343
I am, ma'am. Tom's a
journalist now, Grandmama.

746
00:45:41,343 --> 00:45:45,183
Oh, well, well. I've heard of those
journeys on my side of the water.

747
00:45:45,783 --> 00:45:48,863
It's very pleasant to hear
of them happening here.

748
00:45:52,023 --> 00:45:55,063
It's all right, Mama. You
can leave us unchaperoned.

749
00:45:55,423 --> 00:45:57,703
After tomorrow, all things are permitted.

750
00:45:57,703 --> 00:45:59,783
Don't embarrass me.

751
00:45:59,783 --> 00:46:01,783
Bye, Matthew.

752
00:46:01,783 --> 00:46:04,143
Get a good night's sleep.

753
00:46:08,783 --> 00:46:11,943
How many moments of Crawley
history has this room seen?

754
00:46:12,583 --> 00:46:14,623
And many more to come.

755
00:46:15,343 --> 00:46:17,343
I hope so.

756
00:46:18,303 --> 00:46:22,143
In fact, what happened in
the search for...Mr Pumpkin?

757
00:46:22,743 --> 00:46:25,143
Swire's heir. Have you heard anything?

758
00:46:25,143 --> 00:46:28,343
Yes, Charkham sent a telegram.
I've got it here, actually.

759
00:46:35,703 --> 00:46:38,823
'Convincing proof of
Pullbrook's death. Stop.

760
00:46:39,423 --> 00:46:41,463
Investigating date.'

761
00:46:42,223 --> 00:46:43,943
What does that mean?

762
00:46:43,943 --> 00:46:48,143
Well, if Pullbrook died after
Reggie then his heirs get the money.

763
00:46:48,143 --> 00:46:50,223
If he died first, then I do.

764
00:46:50,223 --> 00:46:53,583
But that's absurd. What right
have his heirs to inherit anything?

765
00:46:53,583 --> 00:46:56,943
Darling, what right have I? Frankly,
what difference does it make?

766
00:46:56,943 --> 00:46:58,783
I shan't keep it if I get it.

767
00:46:58,783 --> 00:47:01,183
Well, actually, you will.

768
00:47:01,863 --> 00:47:04,503
Because something rather
terrible has happened.

769
00:47:05,863 --> 00:47:10,823
You see, apparently Papa has
lost a great deal of money.

770
00:47:12,223 --> 00:47:14,143
Enough to ruin him.

771
00:47:14,143 --> 00:47:16,103
Enough for us to lose Downton.

772
00:47:16,103 --> 00:47:18,143
God, I'm sorry.

773
00:47:19,223 --> 00:47:24,063
I'm so sorry. Yes, but surely if
Mr Pullbrook did die before Swire,

774
00:47:24,063 --> 00:47:26,103
then we're saved.

775
00:47:27,863 --> 00:47:30,103
Darling, I don't think you understand.

776
00:47:31,863 --> 00:47:34,383
Reggie Swire put me in his
will because he believed

777
00:47:34,383 --> 00:47:36,423
I was his daughter's one true love.

778
00:47:37,223 --> 00:47:39,223
So you were. Yes, but... 

779
00:47:40,863 --> 00:47:44,223
But I broke Lavinia's heart and
she died. He never knew that.

780
00:47:44,983 --> 00:47:48,543
How could I possibly allow myself
to profit from her death to... 

781
00:47:49,343 --> 00:47:51,303
to dine in splendour

782
00:47:51,303 --> 00:47:53,583
because I took away a woman's will to live?

783
00:47:53,583 --> 00:47:57,303
So, you're prepared to destroy us
in payment for your destroying her?

784
00:47:57,303 --> 00:48:01,063
Darling, please. You know I
would do anything for this family.

785
00:48:01,063 --> 00:48:02,943
Anything except help us.

786
00:48:02,943 --> 00:48:07,063
Except save Papa from living out
his days in humiliation and grief.

787
00:48:07,063 --> 00:48:10,063
And what about us? What about our children?

788
00:48:10,063 --> 00:48:12,463
God, Matthew, how can
you be so disappointing?

789
00:48:12,463 --> 00:48:15,303
Mary, please. No! Don't
you see what this means?

790
00:48:15,303 --> 00:48:17,703
Don't you see what a difference this makes?

791
00:48:17,703 --> 00:48:20,423
It means that you're
not on our side, Matthew.

792
00:48:20,423 --> 00:48:23,063
It means that deep down
you're not on our side!

793
00:48:50,075 --> 00:48:52,675
How are you getting on
with your new companion?

794
00:48:52,675 --> 00:48:55,755
I don't like him, but so
far I've kept it to myself.

795
00:48:57,235 --> 00:48:59,955
So, who are the bridesmaids?

796
00:49:00,955 --> 00:49:03,555
You don't care about
all that? You're wrong.

797
00:49:03,555 --> 00:49:05,635
It's the stuff of my dreams.

798
00:49:05,955 --> 00:49:09,035
The panic that a dinner won't be
ready, or a frock isn't ironed,

799
00:49:09,035 --> 00:49:11,035
or a gun wasn't cleaned.

800
00:49:11,115 --> 00:49:13,555
Do you know where you're
going for the honeymoon?

801
00:49:13,555 --> 00:49:15,515
I want to talk about that.

802
00:49:15,515 --> 00:49:18,715
They'll stay in London with Lady
Rosamund for a couple of days,

803
00:49:18,715 --> 00:49:20,715
just to get used to each other... 

804
00:49:21,435 --> 00:49:23,555
and then they go to the South of France.

805
00:49:24,155 --> 00:49:28,195
I'll hire replacements in London
and then I'll come home instead.

806
00:49:29,115 --> 00:49:31,155
Lady Mary won't mind. I'll pay.

807
00:49:31,155 --> 00:49:33,235
Why would you do that?

808
00:49:33,235 --> 00:49:35,395
Well, to be near you, of course.

809
00:49:35,395 --> 00:49:37,435
Don't you understand?

810
00:49:37,435 --> 00:49:40,675
While I'm in here you have to
live my life as well as your own.

811
00:49:40,675 --> 00:49:45,475
Go to France, see some
sites, get us some memories.

812
00:49:45,915 --> 00:49:47,715
But I wouldn't be home for a month.

813
00:49:47,715 --> 00:49:49,875
Won't we have something to talk about?

814
00:49:49,875 --> 00:49:51,875
Go. I insist.

815
00:49:53,275 --> 00:49:55,275
For my sake.

816
00:49:56,635 --> 00:49:58,635
I was so afraid I was going to be late.

817
00:49:58,955 --> 00:50:00,955
Ah ha ha.

818
00:50:01,675 --> 00:50:03,675
Violet.

819
00:50:06,955 --> 00:50:08,795
Oh, dear.

820
00:50:08,795 --> 00:50:12,075
I'm afraid the war has
made old women of us both.

821
00:50:12,075 --> 00:50:16,555
Oh, I wouldn't say that. But
then, I always keep out of the sun.

822
00:50:17,435 --> 00:50:19,635
How do you find Downton on your return?

823
00:50:19,635 --> 00:50:21,475
Much the same, really.

824
00:50:21,475 --> 00:50:24,475
Probably too much the same,
but I don't want to cast a pall

825
00:50:24,475 --> 00:50:27,995
over all the happiness.
How could you ever do that?

826
00:50:29,155 --> 00:50:31,835
Tell me, what do you
think of young Lochinvar

827
00:50:31,835 --> 00:50:35,515
who has so ably carried off
our granddaughter and our money?

828
00:50:35,515 --> 00:50:38,235
Do you approve of him?
Not as much as you will... 

829
00:50:38,235 --> 00:50:40,435
... when you get to know him.

830
00:50:40,435 --> 00:50:44,395
Has he gone home to change? Oh,
no, we won't see him again tonight.

831
00:50:45,315 --> 00:50:49,075
The groom never sees the bride
the night before the wedding.

832
00:50:50,635 --> 00:50:53,155
Nothing ever alters
for you people, does it?

833
00:50:53,155 --> 00:50:56,075
Revolutions erupt and
monarchies crash to the ground

834
00:50:56,075 --> 00:50:58,995
and the groom still cannot see
the bride before the wedding.

835
00:50:59,435 --> 00:51:04,195
You Americans never understand
the importance of tradition.

836
00:51:04,835 --> 00:51:07,715
Yes, we do. We just don't
give it power over us.

837
00:51:08,275 --> 00:51:11,435
History and tradition took
Europe into a world war.

838
00:51:11,995 --> 00:51:14,755
Maybe you should think
about letting go of its hand.

839
00:51:20,115 --> 00:51:22,075
There you are.

840
00:51:22,075 --> 00:51:24,395
I see you've said hello to Grandmama.

841
00:51:24,395 --> 00:51:26,515
She is like a homing pigeon.

842
00:51:28,195 --> 00:51:31,235
She finds our underbelly every time.

843
00:51:32,595 --> 00:51:34,595
Dreadful woman.

844
00:51:34,715 --> 00:51:37,875
No, it wasn't me.
Someone sent Sybil and Tom

845
00:51:37,875 --> 00:51:39,755
the price for the tickets to come over.

846
00:51:39,755 --> 00:51:43,515
Does it matter who it was? It
meant we could be at the wedding.

847
00:51:43,515 --> 00:51:47,155
Of course I wish it had been
you, Papa. But I don't mind.

848
00:51:47,155 --> 00:51:49,075
I thank them, whoever they are.

849
00:51:49,075 --> 00:51:53,235
Well, I'm very glad you're here, but
it wasn't me either, I'm sad to say.

850
00:51:53,235 --> 00:51:55,995
I love a mystery. Who could it be?

851
00:51:55,995 --> 00:51:59,315
My guess is cousin Isobel. She
always likes to stick her oar in.

852
00:51:59,315 --> 00:52:01,115
I'm going to ask her.

853
00:52:01,115 --> 00:52:03,115
For heaven's sake. It was me.

854
00:52:06,955 --> 00:52:09,435
You? But it wasn't your writing.

855
00:52:09,435 --> 00:52:11,475
No. Smithers did it.

856
00:52:11,475 --> 00:52:14,075
Like all ladies' maids
she lives for intrigue.

857
00:52:14,075 --> 00:52:15,955
You wanted me to come here?

858
00:52:15,955 --> 00:52:20,395
I wanted Sybil and her husband to
be here for Mary's wedding, yes.

859
00:52:21,315 --> 00:52:23,315
Why keep that secret?

860
00:52:23,955 --> 00:52:25,795
Silly, wasn't it?

861
00:52:25,795 --> 00:52:28,195
I'm very touched. I'll admit it.

862
00:52:28,195 --> 00:52:30,475
How democratic.

863
00:52:30,475 --> 00:52:33,075
Makes me think maybe I
have been mistaken in you.

864
00:52:33,515 --> 00:52:35,475
I am a woman of many parts.

865
00:52:36,515 --> 00:52:39,875
After all, Branson is... I mean, Tom... 

866
00:52:40,395 --> 00:52:42,435
You're a member of the family now.

867
00:52:42,435 --> 00:52:44,995
You'll find we Crawleys stick together.

868
00:52:45,675 --> 00:52:49,115
Not always. Mary, what is it?

869
00:52:49,115 --> 00:52:51,115
Nothing, it's just... 

870
00:52:53,515 --> 00:52:55,515
Oh, Mary.

871
00:52:59,835 --> 00:53:02,835
Oh, there you are. That's
all for this course.

872
00:53:03,315 --> 00:53:05,315
Don't you think, Daisy?

873
00:53:05,835 --> 00:53:08,395
Is Daisy all right, Mrs Patmore? Oh, yes.

874
00:53:08,395 --> 00:53:10,435
She's been such a big help.

875
00:53:10,435 --> 00:53:13,795
Now, I think we should
check the pudding, Daisy.

876
00:53:13,795 --> 00:53:15,835
Don't you agree?

877
00:53:15,835 --> 00:53:19,675
It's nerves. Everyone cries at
some point before their wedding.

878
00:53:19,675 --> 00:53:21,755
But what was the quarrel about?

879
00:53:21,755 --> 00:53:25,755
I'm not sure. I know she accused
him of not being on our side.

880
00:53:25,755 --> 00:53:27,595
Oh, well, I hope she's wrong.

881
00:53:27,595 --> 00:53:29,355
That could be rather serious.

882
00:53:29,355 --> 00:53:32,795
Of course he's on our side. It's
ridiculous. I'll go and see him.

883
00:53:32,795 --> 00:53:36,835
No, I'll go. I'm his best
man. I should be the one to go.

884
00:53:37,115 --> 00:53:40,635
What? I know what it is
to marry into this family.

885
00:53:40,635 --> 00:53:42,755
I'm not comparing myself to Mr Crawley,

886
00:53:42,755 --> 00:53:45,315
but he is another kind of
outsider. Well, I hardly... 

887
00:53:45,315 --> 00:53:47,715
Well, why not? He's the
one who'll lose his job

888
00:53:47,715 --> 00:53:49,715
if the wedding's cancelled.

889
00:53:53,835 --> 00:53:57,115
I see what you're doing,
you know? What's that, then?

890
00:53:57,635 --> 00:53:59,875
Not responding to my protest.

891
00:53:59,875 --> 00:54:02,355
Oh... 'Not responding to my protest.'

892
00:54:02,355 --> 00:54:04,395
Very elegant, I must say.

893
00:54:04,395 --> 00:54:06,235
Who've you been talking to?

894
00:54:06,235 --> 00:54:08,355
Thomas? Well... 

895
00:54:10,435 --> 00:54:12,555
Oh, just give me the cloth and I'll dry.

896
00:54:18,995 --> 00:54:22,475
Suppose he never gets the
money... It's not about the money.

897
00:54:22,475 --> 00:54:25,035
It's that he won't save Papa when he could.

898
00:54:26,835 --> 00:54:28,875
But he has to be true to himself.

899
00:54:28,875 --> 00:54:32,715
That's the point. He puts
himself above the rest of us.

900
00:54:32,715 --> 00:54:34,715
Don't you see?

901
00:54:35,155 --> 00:54:37,955
What I see is a good man, my lady.

902
00:54:38,995 --> 00:54:40,995
And they're not like buses.

903
00:54:40,995 --> 00:54:43,995
There won't be another one
along in ten minutes' time.

904
00:54:54,875 --> 00:54:56,875
It seems big, but it's not big.

905
00:54:58,595 --> 00:55:00,555
And if it happens?

906
00:55:00,555 --> 00:55:02,675
I get the money.

907
00:55:02,675 --> 00:55:04,675
I can't do what she wants.

908
00:55:05,235 --> 00:55:09,515
It's strange for me to be arguing about
inherited money and saving estates.

909
00:55:09,635 --> 00:55:12,915
When the old me would like to
put a bomb under the lot of you.

910
00:55:13,395 --> 00:55:16,835
But? But you're meant to be together.

911
00:55:17,795 --> 00:55:20,475
I've known that as long
as I've been at the house.

912
00:55:20,475 --> 00:55:23,875
And at first this kept you apart
and then that kept you apart.

913
00:55:25,395 --> 00:55:27,475
But please don't risk it a third time.

914
00:55:28,275 --> 00:55:30,275
Because I'll tell you this.

915
00:55:30,675 --> 00:55:34,195
You won't be happy with anyone else
while Lady Mary walks the earth.

916
00:55:34,795 --> 00:55:37,515
Call her Mary, please.
Never mind what I call her.

917
00:55:38,315 --> 00:55:42,355
I know what I'll call you if you let
this chance slip through your fingers.

918
00:55:56,115 --> 00:55:59,755
I just need a word. No. Go away.

919
00:55:59,755 --> 00:56:01,915
I'm undressed. You can't come in.

920
00:56:01,915 --> 00:56:04,435
One word. Come to the door.

921
00:56:04,435 --> 00:56:07,315
Please. Just give him this chance.

922
00:56:07,995 --> 00:56:10,715
I won't look at you. It'd
be unlucky if you did.

923
00:56:11,435 --> 00:56:13,435
Only if we were getting married.

924
00:56:14,075 --> 00:56:16,075
Which we are.

925
00:56:26,355 --> 00:56:29,755
My darling, I refuse to quarrel
about something that hasn't happened

926
00:56:29,755 --> 00:56:31,515
and probably never will.

927
00:56:31,515 --> 00:56:33,515
That's what Anna says.

928
00:56:33,995 --> 00:56:35,995
Then she's right.

929
00:56:37,115 --> 00:56:41,875
My darling, I'm sure we will fight
about money and about Downton,

930
00:56:42,835 --> 00:56:45,755
about how to rear our children,
about any number of things.

931
00:56:45,755 --> 00:56:48,595
Then shouldn't we accept it?

932
00:56:48,595 --> 00:56:50,955
Matthew, I've been thinking... 

933
00:56:51,555 --> 00:56:53,875
And I'm not angry now, truly I'm not.

934
00:56:54,755 --> 00:56:58,195
But if we can disagree over
something as fundamental as this,

935
00:56:58,195 --> 00:57:01,595
then shouldn't we be
brave and back away now?

936
00:57:02,275 --> 00:57:04,075
No.

937
00:57:04,075 --> 00:57:07,675
It's not because you're afraid of
calling it off? Because I'm not.

938
00:57:07,675 --> 00:57:09,675
No.

939
00:57:10,195 --> 00:57:12,195
It's because of something Tom said.

940
00:57:15,075 --> 00:57:19,195
That I would never be happy with anyone
else as long as you walked the earth.

941
00:57:19,475 --> 00:57:21,475
Which is true.

942
00:57:22,715 --> 00:57:26,715
And I think you feel the same about me.

943
00:57:34,635 --> 00:57:36,635
Can I kiss you?

944
00:57:37,875 --> 00:57:39,875
Because I need to.

945
00:57:40,715 --> 00:57:44,235
Very much. No. It's bad luck to look at me.

946
00:57:45,195 --> 00:57:47,515
What about if I close
my eyes and you do too?

947
00:57:51,915 --> 00:57:53,955
All right.

948
00:57:53,955 --> 00:57:55,955
But you mustn't cheat.

949
00:58:23,755 --> 00:58:25,755
Good night.

950
00:58:49,477 --> 00:58:51,797
Right, I'm off to collect Matthew.

951
00:58:51,797 --> 00:58:55,997
You look very smart. I hope so.
Because I'm extremely uncomfortable.

952
00:58:57,357 --> 00:58:59,357
Branson... 

953
00:59:00,477 --> 00:59:02,477
That is, Tom.

954
00:59:03,757 --> 00:59:07,597
I want to thank you for what you did
last night. I'm grateful. I mean it.

955
00:59:07,597 --> 00:59:09,397
They're both strong characters.

956
00:59:09,397 --> 00:59:13,317
I'd say we have plenty of slamming
doors and shouting matches to come.

957
00:59:14,757 --> 00:59:16,757
Forgive me, I was about to be indignant,

958
00:59:16,757 --> 00:59:19,277
but you have a perfect
right to speak as you do.

959
00:59:19,557 --> 00:59:21,477
I hope you mean that too.

960
00:59:21,477 --> 00:59:23,437
I do.

961
00:59:23,437 --> 00:59:25,437
Now, hurry up.

962
00:59:26,837 --> 00:59:30,557
You'd ask if there was anything
you wanted me to tell you.

963
00:59:30,917 --> 00:59:34,237
I mean, I'm sure you
know. More than you did.

964
00:59:34,237 --> 00:59:37,397
And relax. There isn't
anything I need to hear now.

965
00:59:37,397 --> 00:59:41,437
Because when two people love each
other, you understand, everything... 

966
00:59:43,997 --> 00:59:45,997
It's the most terrific fun.

967
00:59:46,557 --> 00:59:48,597
Careful, Mama, or you'll shock Anna.

968
00:59:49,277 --> 00:59:53,797
I'm a married woman now, my
lady. I think we should go.

969
00:59:53,797 --> 00:59:56,677
What about Anna? How are you
going to get to the church?

970
00:59:56,677 --> 00:59:58,677
They're waiting for me in the wagonette.

971
00:59:58,677 --> 01:00:00,677
I'll see you there.

972
01:00:01,997 --> 01:00:04,797
I know mine was a wild
runaway marriage, darling.

973
01:00:05,717 --> 01:00:07,917
And yours is the one everyone wanted.

974
01:00:07,917 --> 01:00:12,437
What's so thrilling is that
this is every bit as romantic.

975
01:00:13,117 --> 01:00:15,757
Thank you, for always being so sweet.

976
01:00:21,917 --> 01:00:26,637
Love and position in one handsome
package. Who could ask for more?

977
01:00:31,357 --> 01:00:33,357
Never mind Edith.

978
01:00:34,997 --> 01:00:39,597
Well, very, very good
luck, my beautiful daughter.

979
01:00:53,717 --> 01:00:58,277
Now, you've a great big porter cab
all to yourselves. Think of that.

980
01:00:58,277 --> 01:01:02,277
So we'll expect you to behave
as if you were quite grown up.

981
01:01:02,277 --> 01:01:04,277
You can do that, can't you?

982
01:01:04,957 --> 01:01:06,797
Got everything you need? Yes.

983
01:01:06,797 --> 01:01:08,797
Hop in, then.

984
01:01:09,077 --> 01:01:11,077
Careful with your dress.

985
01:01:13,437 --> 01:01:15,317
Settle down.

986
01:01:15,317 --> 01:01:17,317
All right?

987
01:01:18,957 --> 01:01:21,237
Don't do anything I wouldn't do. Bye.

988
01:01:23,357 --> 01:01:26,197
Bye-bye. Good luck!

989
01:01:36,957 --> 01:01:38,957
Have you got everything you need? We do.

990
01:01:38,957 --> 01:01:41,877
Now, be off with you and enjoy yourself.

991
01:01:42,917 --> 01:01:44,757
I wish we were going.

992
01:01:44,757 --> 01:01:47,477
Who'll get the food ready
for when they come back?

993
01:01:47,637 --> 01:01:50,077
Still, fetch your coat
and we'll see her off.

994
01:01:53,437 --> 01:01:55,677
Right, see you in a minute.

995
01:02:00,357 --> 01:02:03,437
We're just leaving now, my
lord. As soon as we've got Anna.

996
01:02:03,437 --> 01:02:05,437
Here comes the bride.

997
01:02:33,117 --> 01:02:35,117
Will I do, Carson?

998
01:02:37,157 --> 01:02:39,157
Very nicely, my lady.

999
01:02:44,957 --> 01:02:48,397
Thank heavens you got everything
settled. You had me worried.

1000
01:02:48,397 --> 01:02:51,957
It's not quite settled, I'm afraid.
He won't get off that easily.

1001
01:02:52,957 --> 01:02:54,957
But you're happy?

1002
01:02:54,957 --> 01:02:56,957
I am.

1003
01:02:57,437 --> 01:02:59,437
And what about you?

1004
01:03:01,357 --> 01:03:07,037
I'm so happy, so very happy,
I feel my chest will explode.

1005
01:03:25,717 --> 01:03:28,157
It's so lovely that you're
here. Come behind us.

1006
01:03:28,157 --> 01:03:31,317
I can't. I'm not family.
But you almost are.

1007
01:03:34,357 --> 01:03:36,757
Morning. Thank you.

1008
01:03:39,597 --> 01:03:42,957
Molesley, I'm very grateful to you

1009
01:03:42,957 --> 01:03:44,997
for keeping Mr Branson up to the mark.

1010
01:03:45,397 --> 01:03:48,277
We both are, aren't we? We certainly are.

1011
01:03:49,037 --> 01:03:51,037
Thank you, sir.

1012
01:03:56,957 --> 01:03:59,557
This is a proud day, Mrs Hughes.

1013
01:04:00,157 --> 01:04:04,117
I don't know if I'm proud, but I'm
very glad you're happy, Mr Carson.

1014
01:04:05,197 --> 01:04:08,437
You're next, darling, you'll see. Will I?

1015
01:04:08,437 --> 01:04:11,637
So encouraging to see the future unfurl.

1016
01:04:13,437 --> 01:04:18,277
As long as you remember it will
bear no resemblance to the past.

1017
01:05:07,717 --> 01:05:09,717
Good luck.

1018
01:05:46,357 --> 01:05:48,357
You came.

1019
01:05:49,117 --> 01:05:51,837
To be honest, I wasn't
completely sure you would.

1020
01:05:52,157 --> 01:05:55,837
I'm glad to hear it. I
should hate to be predictable.

1021
01:05:59,917 --> 01:06:02,397
I'm sorry, it still seems
odd to be found in your bed.

1022
01:06:02,397 --> 01:06:05,557
Isn't it dangerous to let this
Strallan nonsense simmer on?

1023
01:06:05,557 --> 01:06:07,317
Ask him to end it.

1024
01:06:07,317 --> 01:06:10,037
Somehow the poor chap managed
to burn a hole in my tails.

1025
01:06:10,037 --> 01:06:12,837
This is what comes of making
him run before he could walk.

1026
01:06:12,837 --> 01:06:14,957
We've 20 lords and
ladies in the drawing room

1027
01:06:14,957 --> 01:06:16,957
and we've got no dinner to give them.

1028
01:06:24,917 --> 01:06:28,837
His lordship's in trouble. He thinks
he may have to sell. Sell Downton?

1029
01:06:29,677 --> 01:06:31,677
itfc subtitles

